[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste-ready recipe** that shows how to:

1. **Load a PMTiles file** (a single-file container for vector tiles) in the browser.  
2. **Register the `pmtiles://` protocol** that MapLibre GL JS understands.  
3. **Add the PMTiles file as a vector source** to a MapLibre map.  
4. **Create a line layer** that draws features from that source and style it (color, width, dash, etc.).

You can run the example as a single HTML file, or split the pieces into separate modules if you prefer a bundler (Webpack, Vite, etc.). All the libraries are available from public CDNs, so no build step is required.

---

## 1️⃣  HTML Boiler-plate

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles – Line Layer Demo</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
  <div id="map"></div>

  <!-- --------------------------------------------------- -->
  <!-- 2️⃣  Load the libraries (MapLibre + PMTiles) from CDN   -->
  <!-- --------------------------------------------------- -->
  <script type="module">
    // ---- 2.1 Import the two libraries from CDN ----
    import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
    import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

    // --------------------------------------------------- //
    // 3️⃣  Register the `pmtiles://` protocol with MapLibre //
    // --------------------------------------------------- //
    // The PMTiles library implements a fetch-like handler that
    // reads the .pmtiles file (which can be local, on a CDN,
    // or even a Blob URL).  We give it a name ("mytiles") and
    // then use that name in the source URL:  pmtiles://mytiles
    const pmtilesUrl = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/your-org/your-repo/main/data/example.pmtiles';
    // (Replace the URL above with the actual location of your .pmtiles file.)

    // Create a PMTiles object – it will lazily fetch the header and
    // then individual tiles as the map asks for them.
    const pmtiles = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);

    // Register a custom protocol handler.  MapLibre will call this
    // function whenever it sees a URL that starts with "pmtiles://".
    // The handler must return a Promise that resolves to a Response.
    maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => {
      // request.url looks like "pmtiles://mytiles/12/3456/7890.pbf"
      // The PMTiles library expects just the path part after the scheme.
      const path = request.url.replace(/^pmtiles:\/\//, '');
      return pmtiles.get(path);
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------- //
    // 4️⃣  Initialise the MapLibre map                     //
    // --------------------------------------------------- //
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {
        version: 8,
        sources: {
          // A simple background source – you can replace this with any
          // raster or vector basemap you like.
          'osm': {
            type: 'raster',
            tiles: [
              'https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
              'https://b.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
              'https://c.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
            ],
            tileSize: 256,
            attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
          }
        },
        layers: [
          {
            id: 'osm',
            type: 'raster',
            source: 'osm'
          }
        ]
      },
      center: [0, 0],   // lon, lat – adjust to your data’s extent
      zoom: 2
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------- //
    // 5️⃣  Add the PMTiles vector source once the map is ready //
    // --------------------------------------------------- //
    map.on('load', () => {
      // ---- 5.1  Add the vector source ----
      map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
        type: 'vector',
        // The URL uses the custom protocol we registered above.
        // The part after the double slash is the name we gave the PMTiles object.
        // (You can use any name you like – just keep it consistent.)
        url: 'pmtiles://mytiles'   // <-- “mytiles” is the identifier we used in addProtocol
      });

      // ---- 5.2  Find out the source-layer name(s) ----
      // PMTiles files can contain many layers.  The easiest way to discover them
      // is to ask the source after the first tile has been fetched.
      // (You can also look at the .pmtiles metadata with a tool like `pmtiles info`.)
      map.once('data', (e) => {
        if (e.sourceId === 'my-pmtiles-source' && e.isSourceLoaded) {
          const source = map.getSource('my-pmtiles-source');
          // `source.vectorTileLayerIds` is an array of layer names inside the PMTiles.
          console.log('Layers inside the PMTiles:', source.vectorTileLayerIds);
          // For this demo we assume the first layer is the one we want.
          const layerName = source.vectorTileLayerIds[0];

          // ---- 5.3  Add a line layer that draws from that source ----
          map.addLayer({
            id: 'my-lines',
            type: 'line',
            source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
            'source-layer': layerName,   // <-- name from the PMTiles file
            layout: {
              // Optional: smooth joins between line segments
              'line-join': 'round',
              'line-cap': 'round'
            },
            paint: {
              // ---- Styling the line ----
              // 1️⃣  Base colour (hex, rgb, hsl, etc.)
              'line-color': '#ff6600',

              // 2️⃣  Width – you can make it zoom-dependent
              //    The expression below says: 2 px at z = 5, 8 px at z = 12, then stay.
              'line-width': [
                'interpolate',
                ['linear'],
                ['zoom'],
                5, 2,
                12, 8
              ],

              // 3️⃣  Opacity (0 = transparent, 1 = opaque)
              'line-opacity': 0.9,

              // 4️⃣  Optional dash pattern (array of dash/space lengths in pixels)
              //    Comment out if you want a solid line.
              // 'line-dasharray': [2, 4],

              // 5️⃣  Optional blur (good for glow effects)
              // 'line-blur': 1
            }
          });

          // OPTIONAL: Fit the map to the extent of the line data
          // (requires the source to have a `bounds` property – many PMTiles do)
          if (source.bounds) {
            map.fitBounds(source.bounds, { padding: 40 });
          }
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 📚 What’s Going on? (Step-by-step explanation)

| Step | What you do | Why it matters |
|------|-------------|----------------|
| **2** | Import **MapLibre GL JS** (`maplibregl`) and the **PMTiles** helper (`PMTiles`). | MapLibre renders the map; PMTiles knows how to read a `.pmtiles` container. |
| **3** | Create a `PMTiles` instance pointing at your `.pmtiles` file and register a **custom protocol handler** (`pmtiles://`). | MapLibre’s `addSource` expects a URL it can `fetch`. The handler intercepts those URLs and returns the tile data from the single file. |
| **4** | Initialise the map with a base style (here a simple OSM raster). | You need a map canvas before you can add layers. |
| **5.1** | `map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {type:'vector', url:'pmtiles://mytiles'})`. | This tells MapLibre “there’s a vector source, get its tiles via the `pmtiles://` protocol”. |
| **5.2** | After the source loads, read `source.vectorTileLayerIds` to discover the **source-layer name(s)** inside the PMTiles. | Vector tiles can contain many layers (e.g., `roads`, `buildings`). You must specify which one you want to draw. |
| **5.3** | `map.addLayer({ id:'my-lines', type:'line', source:'my-pmtiles-source', 'source-layer': layerName, paint:{…}})`. | This creates the visual line layer and applies styling (color, width, dash, opacity, etc.). |
| **Optional** | `map.fitBounds(source.bounds)` | Zooms the map to the data’s extent automatically. |

---

## 🎯 Customising the Line Style

All line-specific paint properties are documented in the MapLibre style spec. Below are the most common ones you can tweak:

| Paint property | Example value | Effect |
|----------------|---------------|--------|
| `line-color` | `'#00bfff'` or `'rgb(0,191,255)'` | Line colour |
| `line-width` | `['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 5, 1, 12, 6]` | Width that changes with zoom |
| `line-opacity` | `0.7` | Transparency |
| `line-dasharray` | `[4, 2]` | Dashed line (4 px dash, 2 px gap) |
| `line-cap` | `'round'` | End caps (`butt`, `round`, `square`) |
| `line-join` | `'round'` | Corner joins (`bevel`, `round`, `miter`) |
| `line-blur` | `1` | Soft glow around the line |
| `line-gradient` | `['interpolate', ['linear'], ['line-progress'], 0, 'red', 1, 'blue']` | Gradient along the line length |

You can combine any of these in the `paint` object. The example above already shows a zoom-dependent width and a solid orange colour.

---

## 🛠️ Debugging Tips

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No line appears** | Wrong `source-layer` name or source never loaded. | Open the browser console – you should see the array printed by `console.log(source.vectorTileLayerIds)`. Use the exact string from that array. |
| **Tiles 404 / CORS errors** | The PMTiles URL is on a different origin without CORS headers. | Host the `.pmtiles` file on a CDN that sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (most public CDNs do). If you serve it yourself, add that header. |
| **Map freezes while loading** | Large PMTiles file and no `async` handling. | The PMTiles library streams tiles on demand, so it’s already async. If you still see a freeze, make sure you’re not loading the file via a synchronous XHR elsewhere. |
| **Line looks too thin at high zoom** | `line-width` not zoom-dependent. | Use an `interpolate` expression as shown in the example. |
| **Dotted line looks jagged** | `line-cap`/`line-join` not set to `round`. | Add `layout: { 'line-cap': 'round', 'line-join': 'round' }`. |

