## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles – line layer demo</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS (CSS + JS) -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <!-- PMTiles library (provides the pmtiles:// protocol) -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@2.7.0/dist/pmtiles.min.js"></script>

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Register the pmtiles:// protocol
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // The PMTiles library ships a tiny “protocol handler” that MapLibre can call.
  // It works by creating a PMTiles object that knows how to fetch tiles from
  // the .pmtiles file (via fetch, Range requests, or an in-memory blob).

  // Replace this URL with the location of your own .pmtiles file.
  const PMTILES_URL = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/your-org/your-repo/main/data/my-tiles.pmtiles';

  // Create the handler – it will lazily download the file and serve tile blobs.
  const pmtiles = new pmtiles.PMTiles(PMTILES_URL);

  // Register the protocol with MapLibre's internal request manager.
  // After this, any source that uses url: "pmtiles://my-tiles.pmtiles"
  // will be resolved by the handler above.
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => {
    // `request` is an object { url, method, headers, ... }
    // The handler returns a Promise that resolves to a Response-like object.
    return pmtiles.get(request);
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Initialise the MapLibre map
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      // A minimal style – you can replace this with any style URL you like.
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // A simple raster basemap (OpenStreetMap tiles via a free CDN)
        'osm': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'osm',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'osm'
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (lon, lat)
    zoom: 12,
    hash: true
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Add the PMTiles vector source & line layer (once the map is ready)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', async () => {
    // ---- 4a.  Optional: inspect the PMTiles metadata to discover source-layer names ----
    // This is handy if you don’t already know the exact name of the layer inside the file.
    // The metadata call is cheap – it just reads the header of the .pmtiles file.
    const meta = await pmtiles.getMetadata();
    console.log('PMTiles metadata →', meta);
    // `meta` contains a `json` field with the TileJSON that the file was created from.
    // Example: meta.json.vector_layers = [{ id: "roads", ... }, …]
    // Grab the first vector layer name (or pick the one you need):
    const sourceLayer = (meta?.json?.vector_layers?.[0]?.id) || 'my_layer_name';

    // ---- 4b.  Register the vector source ----
    map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
      type: 'vector',
      // The URL uses the custom protocol we registered above.
      // The part after "pmtiles://" can be any string – it is just passed to the handler.
      url: 'pmtiles://' + PMTILES_URL
    });

    // ---- 4c.  Add a line layer that draws from that source ----
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
      // The name of the layer inside the vector tiles (see step 4a).
      'source-layer': sourceLayer,

      // ---- 4d.  Styling the line ----
      paint: {
        // A nice blue line that gets thicker when you zoom in.
        'line-color': '#1E90FF',
        'line-width': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['zoom'],
          10, 1,
          14, 3,
          18, 8
        ],
        // Optional: make the line semi-transparent
        'line-opacity': 0.8,
        // Optional: add a subtle halo (outline) so the line stands out on any basemap
        'line-halo-color': '#ffffff',
        'line-halo-width': 1
      },

      // ---- 4e.  Optional: filter to only show certain features ----
      // Example: only show roads that are classified as "primary" or "secondary"
      // filter: ['in', ['get', 'class'], ['literal', ['primary', 'secondary']]]
    });

    console.log('✅ PMTiles source + line layer added');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 📚 What each part does

| Section | Why it matters |
|---------|----------------|
| **PMTiles library (`pmtiles.min.js`)** | Supplies a tiny fetch-handler that can read a `.pmtiles` file via HTTP Range requests (or from a Blob). |
| `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', …)` | Registers the custom `pmtiles://` URL scheme with MapLibre so the map can request tiles from the handler. |
| **MapLibre map init** | Starts a map with a simple raster basemap (you can swap in any style you like). |
| `pmtiles.getMetadata()` | Reads the TileJSON stored inside the PMTiles file. It tells you the **source-layer names**, the **vector-layer schema**, min/max zoom, etc. |
| `map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {type:'vector', url:'pmtiles://…'})` | Declares a vector source that points at the PMTiles file. The URL uses the protocol we just registered. |
| `map.addLayer({type:'line', source:'my-pmtiles-source', 'source-layer': …})` | Creates a line layer that pulls geometries from the vector source. |
| `paint` block | Controls the visual appearance (color, width, opacity, halo). The `interpolate` expression makes the line width responsive to zoom. |
| Optional `filter` | Lets you narrow the data (e.g., only highways, only features with a certain property). |

---

## 🎯 How to adapt this to **your own data**

1. **Upload your `.pmtiles` file** somewhere it can be fetched with a normal HTTP GET (GitHub raw, S3, Cloudflare R2, your own server, etc.).  
   *Make sure the server supports **Range** requests – they are required for the library to fetch individual tiles without downloading the whole file.*  

2. **Find the source-layer name** inside the file.  
   - The quickest way is the `pmtiles.getMetadata()` call shown above – open the browser console and look at `meta.json.vector_layers`.  
   - If you created the PMTiles with **Tippecanoe**, the layer name is whatever you passed to `--layer=`.  
   - If you used **ogr2ogr** or **tilemaker**, check the TileJSON that those tools output.  

3. **Swap the URL** (`PMTILES_URL`) and the `sourceLayer` variable in the script.  

4. **Tweak the style**:  
   - Change `'line-color'` to any CSS color or an expression that varies by a feature property (`['get', 'type']`).  
   - Adjust the `line-width` stops to suit your map’s zoom range.  
   - Add `line-dasharray`, `line-cap`, `line-join`, etc., if you need a different look.  

5. **Add more layers** (polygons, symbols, etc.) by repeating `map.addLayer` with `type: 'fill'` or `type: 'symbol'` and pointing at the same source.

---

## 🛠️ Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Map shows nothing, console error “Failed to load source”** | The PMTiles URL is wrong, or the server blocks CORS / Range requests. | Verify the URL works in a browser (you should get a 200 with `Accept-Ranges: bytes`). If you see a CORS error, add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on the server or host the file on a CORS-friendly CDN. |
| **`source-layer` not found** | You used the wrong layer name. | Open the dev console, run `pmtiles.getMetadata()` and read `meta.json.vector_layers`. Use the exact `id` string. |
| **Lines appear as a thick solid block** | The line width is too large for the zoom level, or the source geometry is not a line (e.g., polygons). | Double-check the geometry type in the TileJSON (`vector_layers[].geometry_type`). If it’s `Polygon`, use a `fill` layer instead. |
| **Tiles load very slowly** | The server does not support HTTP Range requests, causing the whole file to be downloaded each time. | Move the file to a server that does support Range (most static file hosts do). |
| **MapLibre throws “Invalid value for property line-width”** | The `line-width` expression is malformed. | Ensure the expression array is correctly nested (as in the example). Use the MapLibre style spec for reference. |

